WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.084
Welcome to Lecture 7 of the

2
00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:04.860
FrontierCS 2 course, on machine learning.

3
00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.765
FrontierCS 2 takes a research paper

4
00:00:07.836 --> 00:00:09.610
removes its key idea from

5
00:00:09.681 --> 00:00:11.242
the authors' own code

6
00:00:11.242 --> 00:00:14.010
and asks an AI agent to write it back.

7
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.188
This field has nine tasks

8
00:00:16.188 --> 00:00:19.000
and we visit all of them in four parts.

9
00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.367
We start with the hardest task

10
00:00:21.435 --> 00:00:23.990
in the whole benchmark: KV Compaction.

11
00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:26.933
A language model that reads a long

12
00:00:27.003 --> 00:00:29.992
document keeps a KV cache: for every token

13
00:00:29.992 --> 00:00:33.050
in every layer and head, a key and a value.

14
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.720
The cache grows with the document.

15
00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:39.037
Attention Matching, from ICML 2026

16
00:00:39.037 --> 00:00:41.664
shrinks it one head at a time

17
00:00:41.664 --> 00:00:43.190
with no training.

18
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.220
Our toy head has forty-eight article keys

19
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.900
plus four template keys that always stay.

20
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:51.352
We measure a small cache by its

21
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.250
output gap on test queries.

22
00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:55.590
It works in three steps.

23
00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.391
First, keep the keys that draw the most

24
00:00:58.454 --> 00:01:00.340
attention from sample queries.

25
00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.098
Keeping six of forty-eight this way

26
00:01:03.098 --> 00:01:05.773
the gap falls from zero point nine six

27
00:01:05.773 --> 00:01:07.178
for a random choice

28
00:01:07.178 --> 00:01:08.850
to zero point two eight.

29
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.618
Second, give each kept key a bias

30
00:01:11.689 --> 00:01:14.533
so that together they draw the attention

31
00:01:14.604 --> 00:01:17.520
of the whole article: zero point two two.

32
00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.786
Third, fit their values by least squares

33
00:01:20.786 --> 00:01:22.777
so that the output matches

34
00:01:22.777 --> 00:01:24.270
zero point one four.

35
00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.326
The real task compacts the cache of a Qwen

36
00:01:27.385 --> 00:01:29.448
model with four billion parameters

37
00:01:29.507 --> 00:01:31.511
which then answers multiple-choice

38
00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.574
questions on long stories from the

39
00:01:33.633 --> 00:01:34.930
compacted cache alone.

40
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.720
The cache may keep up to

41
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.160
a tenth of the article.

42
00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:39.031
To pass

43
00:01:39.096 --> 00:01:41.896
every article must stay within one question

44
00:01:41.961 --> 00:01:44.110
of the reference's correct count.

45
00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:45.945
And at the full budget

46
00:01:45.945 --> 00:01:48.528
the compaction must be strictly faster

47
00:01:48.528 --> 00:01:49.820
with no more bytes.

48
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.706
Pause and predict.

49
00:01:51.779 --> 00:01:54.610
Claude Opus 5.5 was about a hundred and

50
00:01:54.682 --> 00:01:57.440
eight times faster than the reference.

51
00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:00.002
On average it answered thirteen point

52
00:02:00.062 --> 00:02:01.616
two questions per article

53
00:02:01.616 --> 00:02:03.230
against twelve point nine.

54
00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:04.540
Did it pass?

55
00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:08.890
No.

56
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.400
It fell more than one question short

57
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.560
on two of the twenty-four articles.

58
00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:16.870
A pass is decided article by article.

59
00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.750
Opus's run was pure key selection.

60
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.257
It scored each key by its attention

61
00:02:23.318 --> 00:02:25.229
from the article's own queries

62
00:02:25.229 --> 00:02:28.002
spread the kept tokens over the whole story

63
00:02:28.002 --> 00:02:30.220
and gave each layer its own budget.

64
00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.347
It kept about three times the

65
00:02:32.408 --> 00:02:33.879
target's article tokens

66
00:02:33.879 --> 00:02:36.330
still below the reference's cache size.

67
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.582
Four of sixteen models scored

68
00:02:38.648 --> 00:02:39.960
above the reference.

69
00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:41.430
None passed.

70
00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.680
So: keep the keys that draw attention

71
00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.210
and fit biases and values to match.

72
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:48.610
And in judging

73
00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:50.920
every article counts on its own.

74
00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.226
Next: building the batches for

75
00:02:53.291 --> 00:02:54.790
reinforcement learning.
